libavcodec/libvorbisenc: accept frames of any size

The encoder required frames of 64 samples (AVCodecContext.frame_size) and
returned at most one packet for each call. ffmpeg 9 cuts a stream into frames
of the size an encoder states in a synchronisation queue and hands each to the
encoder thread through a queue of two, so every 64 samples cost a hand-over
between two threads. For 120 s of 44.1 kHz stereo that is 235,000 futex calls
and 85,000 voluntary context switches, and the encoder thread waits for its
input for most of the run.

Receive packets through the receive_packet callback, so that one call can
return every packet the samples of a frame complete, and drop frame_size. The
encoder then takes the frames the decoder makes (VARIABLE_FRAME_SIZE).

The packets libvorbis writes depend on how the samples are split across its
vorbis_analysis_wrote() calls (a stream encoded from calls of 128 samples
differs from one encoded from calls of 64, by some tens of bytes in 875 KB),
so a frame is not passed through whole: it is cut into the calls of 64 samples
that the required frame size made, keeping the rest for the next frame. The
stream is then the one the required frame size gave, whatever the size of the
frames, and the packet timestamps, which AudioFrameQueue takes from the
frames, are unchanged.

The output packet buffer grows when a frame completes more packets than it
holds, where it used to fail with AVERROR_BUG.

Matroska no longer writes a DefaultDuration of 64 samples and a BlockDuration
for each packet, which both came from frame_size.

